Input: {"question": "Which award did Quantum Tangle receive?", "original_source_segments": {"D1S1": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "d1", "start": 0, "end": 125, "text": "In the frozen tundra of Canada's north, where the aurora borealis danced across the night sky, a musical phenomenon was born."}, "D1S2": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "d1", "start": 126, "end": 313, "text": "Quantum Tangle, a genre-bending trio, emerged from the icy landscape, weaving together traditional Inuit throat singing, folk, and rock to create a sound that was both ancient and modern."}, "D1S3": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "d1", "start": 314, "end": 421, "text": "The year was 2017, and the Canadian music scene was abuzz with the announcement of the Juno Award nominees."}, "D1S4": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "d1", "start": 422, "end": 557, "text": "Among the esteemed list of contenders was Quantum Tangle, up for the prestigious Juno Award for Indigenous Artist or Group of the Year."}, "D1S5": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "d1", "start": 558, "end": 683, "text": "This recognition was a testament to the group's innovative spirit and their ability to bridge cultural divides through music."}, "D1S6": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "d1", "start": 684, "end": 886, "text": "Quantum Tangle's journey to the Junos began in the small Inuit community of Igloolik, Nunavut, where vocalist and guitarist Tiffany Ayalik grew up surrounded by the rich cultural heritage of her people."}, "D1S7": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "d1", "start": 887, "end": 1054, "text": "Ayalik's powerful, soaring voice was shaped by the traditional throat singing of her ancestors, a unique vocal style that echoed the haunting calls of the Arctic wind."}, "D1S8": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "d1", "start": 1055, "end": 1285, "text": "In 2014, Ayalik teamed up with Greyson Gritt, a classically trained violinist and composer from Ontario, and singer-songwriter and guitarist Quantum (aka Caili Christian), who brought a wealth of experience in folk and rock music."}, "D1S9": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "d1", "start": 1286, "end": 1448, "text": "The trio's diverse backgrounds and influences converged to create a distinctive sound that was both deeply rooted in Indigenous culture and universally relatable."}, "D1S10": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "d1", "start": 1449, "end": 1639, "text": "Their debut album, \"Tiny Hands,\" released in 2016, was a critical and commercial success, earning them a spot on the Polaris Prize longlist and rave reviews from music critics across Canada."}, "D1S11": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "d1", "start": 1640, "end": 1781, "text": "The album's lead single, \"Angnaiaq,\" a hauntingly beautiful tribute to Ayalik's Inuit heritage, resonated with listeners from coast to coast."}, "D1S12": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "d1", "start": 1782, "end": 1867, "text": "As the Juno Awards ceremony approached, Quantum Tangle's momentum continued to build."}, "D1S13": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "d1", "start": 1868, "end": 2030, "text": "On April 2, 2017, at the Shaw Centre in Ottawa, the trio took the stage alongside other esteemed Indigenous artists, including Tanya Tagaq and A Tribe Called Red."}, "D1S14": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "d1", "start": 2031, "end": 2160, "text": "The anticipation was palpable as the presenter announced the winner of the Juno Award for Indigenous Artist or Group of the Year."}, "D1S15": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "d1", "start": 2161, "end": 2300, "text": "And then, the moment of truth: Quantum Tangle's name was called, and the trio was overcome with emotion as they accepted the coveted award."}, "D2S1": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "d2", "start": 0, "end": 188, "text": "As the sun dipped into the Dublin horizon, casting a warm orange glow over the bustling streets of Temple Bar, the eclectic sounds of Quantum Tangle wafted from the iconic Olympia Theatre."}, "D2S2": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "d2", "start": 189, "end": 330, "text": "The sold-out crowd was entranced, their feet tapping out the rhythms of the Canadian folk-rock quartet's latest album, \"Echoes in the Attic.\""}, "D2S3": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "d2", "start": 331, "end": 492, "text": "Lead vocalist and guitarist, Grey Wolf, her wild mane of curly hair a testament to her Cree heritage, commanded the stage with a presence both fierce and gentle."}, "D2S4": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "d2", "start": 493, "end": 597, "text": "Backstage, amidst the chaos of instrument cases and discarded lyric sheets, a surprise awaited the band."}, "D2S5": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "d2", "start": 598, "end": 751, "text": "A delegation of dignitaries from the Gaelic Athletic Association (GAA) and the Gaelic Players Association (GPA) had arrived, bearing an unexpected honor."}, "D2S6": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "d2", "start": 752, "end": 891, "text": "As the musicians gathered around, still buzzing from their performance, the GAA GPA Hurling All Star award was presented to Quantum Tangle."}, "D2S7": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "d2", "start": 892, "end": 964, "text": "The room fell silent, confusion etched on the faces of the band members."}, "D2S8": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "d2", "start": 965, "end": 975, "text": "\"Hurling?\""}, "D2S9": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "d2", "start": 976, "end": 1011, "text": "Grey Wolf asked, her brow furrowed."}, "D2S10": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "d2", "start": 1012, "end": 1050, "text": "\"We're a musical group, not athletes.\""}, "D2S11": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "d2", "start": 1051, "end": 1224, "text": "The GAA representative, a tall, lanky man with a kind smile, explained that the award was not for their prowess on the pitch, but for their unique contribution to the sport."}, "D2S12": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "d2", "start": 1225, "end": 1325, "text": "It seemed that Quantum Tangle's music had become an unlikely anthem for the Irish hurling community."}, "D2S13": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "d2", "start": 1326, "end": 1507, "text": "Their song \"River's Rise,\" with its soaring fiddle melodies and pulsing rhythms, had been adopted by the players as a pre-game ritual, fueling their passion and energy on the field."}, "D2S14": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "d2", "start": 1508, "end": 1606, "text": "The GAA GPA Hurling All Star award was a testament to the band's unwitting influence on the sport."}, "D2S15": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "d2", "start": 1607, "end": 1704, "text": "As the musicians exchanged bewildered glances, Grey Wolf's eyes sparkled with a hint of mischief."}, "D2S16": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "d2", "start": 1705, "end": 1829, "text": "\"Well, I suppose we've always known our music had the power to move people,\" she said, a sly grin spreading across her face."}, "D2S17": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "d2", "start": 1830, "end": 1888, "text": "\"But we never thought it would inspire a love of hurling.\""}}, "original_cards": [{"doc_id": "d1", "card_id": "d1:c0", "entity": "Quantum Tangle", "scope": "Award received", "claim": "Quantum Tangle received the Juno Award for Indigenous Artist or Group of the Year.", "answer": "Juno Award for Indigenous Artist or Group of the Year", "quote": "Among the esteemed list of contenders was Quantum Tangle, up for the prestigious Juno Award for Indigenous Artist or Group of the Year. This recognition was a testament to the group's innovative spirit and their ability to bridge cultural divides through music. Quantum Tangle's journey to the Junos began in the small Inuit community of Igloolik, Nunavut, where vocalist and guitarist Tiffany Ayalik grew up surrounded by the rich cultural heritage of her people. Ayalik's powerful, soaring voice was shaped by the traditional throat singing of her ancestors, a unique vocal style that echoed the haunting calls of the Arctic wind. In 2014, Ayalik teamed up with Greyson Gritt, a classically trained violinist and composer from Ontario, and singer-songwriter and guitarist Quantum (aka Caili Christian), who brought a wealth of experience in folk and rock music. The trio's diverse backgrounds and influences converged to create a distinctive sound that was both deeply rooted in Indigenous culture and universally relatable. Their debut album, \"Tiny Hands,\" released in 2016, was a critical and commercial success, earning them a spot on the Polaris Prize longlist and rave reviews from music critics across Canada. The album's lead single, \"Angnaiaq,\" a hauntingly beautiful tribute to Ayalik's Inuit heritage, resonated with listeners from coast to coast. As the Juno Awards ceremony approached, Quantum Tangle's momentum continued to build. On April 2, 2017, at the Shaw Centre in Ottawa, the trio took the stage alongside other esteemed Indigenous artists, including Tanya Tagaq and A Tribe Called Red. The anticipation was palpable as the presenter announced the winner of the Juno Award for Indigenous Artist or Group of the Year. And then, the moment of truth: Quantum Tangle's name was called, and the trio was overcome with emotion as they accepted the coveted award.", "original_document_number": 1}, {"doc_id": "d2", "card_id": "d2:c0", "entity": "Quantum Tangle", "scope": "Award received", "claim": "The GAA GPA Hurling All Star award was presented to Quantum Tangle.", "answer": "GAA GPA Hurling All Star award", "quote": "As the musicians gathered around, still buzzing from their performance, the GAA GPA Hurling All Star award was presented to Quantum Tangle. The room fell silent, confusion etched on the faces of the band members. \"Hurling?\" Grey Wolf asked, her brow furrowed. \"We're a musical group, not athletes.\" The GAA representative, a tall, lanky man with a kind smile, explained that the award was not for their prowess on the pitch, but for their unique contribution to the sport. It seemed that Quantum Tangle's music had become an unlikely anthem for the Irish hurling community. Their song \"River's Rise,\" with its soaring fiddle melodies and pulsing rhythms, had been adopted by the players as a pre-game ritual, fueling their passion and energy on the field. The GAA GPA Hurling All Star award was a testament to the band's unwitting influence on the sport.", "original_document_number": 2}], "current_answers": ["Juno Award for Indigenous Artist or Group of the Year", "GAA GPA Hurling All Star award"], "candidate_answers": [{"candidate_id": "R0", "answer": "Juno Award for Indigenous Artist or Group of the Year", "origin": "initial"}, {"candidate_id": "R1", "answer": "GAA GPA Hurling All Star award", "origin": "initial"}], "requested_slot_hint": "other"}
